{"name":"Cowardly Custard","hex":"#fbf1c0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cowardly","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Showing cowardice; lacking in courage; weakly fearful."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["In the manner of a coward, cowardlily."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cowardly"},{"term":"custard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of sauce made from milk and eggs (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat, served hot poured over desserts, as a filling for some pies and cakes, or cold and solidified; also used as a base for some savoury dishes, such as quiches, or eaten as a stand-alone dessert; egg custard.","A similar sauce made from milk and cornflour or custard powder (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/custard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cowardy Custard","blocks":[{"text":"Cowardy Custard is a musical revue and was one of the last Noël Coward shows staged during his life. It was devised by Gerald Frow, Alan Strachan and Wendy Toye. A book, also titled Cowardy Custard, was published in connection with the revue, similarly celebrating the Coward image."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1e/Cowardy_custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowardy_Custard","wikidata":"Q5179476"},{"title":"Cowardice","blocks":[{"text":"Cowardice is a characteristic wherein excessive fear prevents an individual from taking a risk or facing danger. It is the opposite of courage. As a label, \"cowardice\" indicates a failure of character in the face of a challenge. One who succumbs to cowardice is known as a coward."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Cowardly_lion2.jpg/330px-Cowardly_lion2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cowardice","wikidata":"Q1401607"},{"title":"Custard","blocks":[{"text":"Custard is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened milk, cheese, or cream cooked with egg or egg yolk to thicken it, and sometimes also flour, corn starch, or gelatin. Depending on the recipe, custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring sauce to the thick pastry cream used to fill éclairs. The most common custards are used in custard desserts or dessert sauces and typically include sugar and vanilla; however, savory custards are also found, e.g., in quiche."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/330px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custard","wikidata":"Q625825"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Dessertcustard.jpg/500px-Dessertcustard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Dessertcustard.jpg/500px-Dessertcustard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A formal custard preparation, garnished with raspberries","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADessertcustard.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Pastry_cream.jpg/500px-Pastry_cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Pastry_cream.jpg/1280px-Pastry_cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Pastry_cream.jpg/500px-Pastry_cream.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pastry cream","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APastry_cream.jpg"},{"srcset